Setting up Laravel Dusk on an AWS EC2 server involves the following high-level steps:

1. Install and configure a web server: Before installing Laravel and Dusk, you'll need to install and configure a web server, such as Apache or Nginx.

2. Create an AWS EC2 instance: If you haven't already, launch a new EC2 instance on AWS with an OS that supports PHP (such as Amazon Linux 2) and update the necessary packages.

3. Install PHP: Install PHP on your EC2 instance by following the instructions for your chosen distribution. For example, with Amazon Linux 2:

    ```
    sudo yum update
    sudo amazon-linux-extras install -y php8.0
    ```

4. Install Laravel: Install Laravel on your EC2 instance using Composer:

    ```
    sudo su
    cd /var/www/html
    composer create-project --prefer-dist laravel/laravel my-project
    ```

5. Install Dusk: Add Dusk to your Laravel project using Composer:

    ```
    composer require --dev laravel/dusk
    ```

6. Install a web driver: Dusk requires a web driver to interact with your application. Choose a driver based on your platform and requirements (e.g., Chrome, Firefox, Selenium), and install it. You can use the following commands to install the Chrome web driver:

    ```
    wget https://chromedriver.storage.googleapis.com/latest/chromedriver_linux64.zip
    unzip chromedriver_linux64.zip
    sudo mv chromedriver /usr/bin/
    ```

7. Configure your Laravel project: In your Laravel project, create a `dusk.php` configuration file in `config/`:

    ```
    <?php

    return [
        'driver' => 'chrome',

        'launchOptions' => [
            'args' => ['headless', 'disable-gpu'],
        ],

        'chrome' => [
            'binary' => '/usr/bin/google-chrome-stable',
        ],

        'phantomjs' => [
            'extensions' => [
                'phantomjs-page.js',
            ],
        ],

        // ...
    ];
    ```

8. Create a new tests/DuskTestCase.php file:

    ```
    <?php

    namespace Tests\Browser;

    use Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\WithFaker;
    use Tests\TestCase;

    class DuskTestCase extends TestCase
    {
        use WithFaker;

        protected $dusk;

        protected function setUp(): void
        {
            parent::setUp();

            $this->ypassAuthentication();
        }

        protected function passAuthentication()
        {
            $this->signInUsingLaravelPassport([
                'email' => 'user@example.com',
                'password' => 'password',
                'remember' => true,
            ]);
        }
    }
    ```

9. Use Dusk in your test case: 

    ```php
    <?php

    namespace Tests\Browser;

    use Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\WithFaker;
    use Tests\TestCase;

    class ExampleTest extends DuskTestCase
    {
        public function test_basic_example()
        {
            $this->browse(function (Browser $browser) {
                $browser->visit('/')->assertSee('Laravel');
            });
        }
    }
    ```

10. Run tests: Run your tests using `vendor/bin/dusk artisan test`.

